Usage: df [OPTION]... [FILE]...

The df command displays the amount of available disk space on file
systems of which the invoking user has adequate read access.

On Windows, it reports information about volumes that contain the
specified paths, including total size, used space, and available space.

OPTIONS:
  -a, --all               include all file systems
  -B, --block-size ARG    scale sizes by SIZE before printing them
  -h, --human-readable    print sizes in powers of 1024 (e.g., 1023M)
  -H, --si                print sizes in powers of 1000 (e.g., 1.1G)
  -i, --inodes            list inode information instead of block usage
  -k                      like --block-size=1K
  -m                      like --block-size=1M
  -l, --local             limit listing to local file systems
  -T, --print-type        print file system type
  -t, --type ARG          limit listing to file systems of type TYPE
  -x, --exclude-type ARG  limit listing to file systems not of type TYPE
      --total             produce a grand total
      --sync              invoke sync before getting usage info
      --no-sync           do not invoke sync before getting usage info
      --output[=ARG]      use the output format defined by FIELD_LIST
  -P, --portability       use the POSIX output format
  -v                      (ignored) [DIFFERS: GNU ignores -v]
      --help              display this help and exit
  -V, --version           output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
